<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05TM">Warning <span data-tag="05TM">7.7.2.9</span>.</a> Beware that the second assertion of Theorem <a data-tag="05TL" href="/tag/05TL">7.7.2.8</a> is generally false if we do not assume either that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ has $K$-indexed colimits or a final object. For example, suppose that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a Kan complex. Then the evaluation map $\operatorname{ev}_{1}: \operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a trivial Kan fibration (Corollary <a data-tag="00UT" href="/tag/00UT">3.1.3.6</a>), so every morphism $F: K^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an (effective) descent diagram for $\operatorname{ev}_{1}$. </p>
